And gripe number two is here. This is not actually a correct change. This should have been foreshadowed by the "funny change" you mention in the OP. More importantly, however, I'd ask that you apply the following diff:

```diff
diff --git a/osu.Game/Rulesets/Mods/ModTimeRamp.cs b/osu.Game/Rulesets/Mods/ModTimeRamp.cs
index 98abda872b..26de1bd346 100644
--- a/osu.Game/Rulesets/Mods/ModTimeRamp.cs
+++ b/osu.Game/Rulesets/Mods/ModTimeRamp.cs
@@ -4,7 +4,10 @@
 using System;
 using System.Linq;
 using osu.Framework.Audio;
+using osu.Framework.Audio.Track;
 using osu.Framework.Bindables;
+using osu.Framework.Logging;
+using osu.Framework.Utils;
 using osu.Game.Beatmaps;
 using osu.Game.Configuration;
 using osu.Game.Rulesets.Objects;
@@ -88,6 +91,9 @@ public double ApplyToRate(double time, double rate = 1)
 
         public virtual void Update(Playfield playfield)
         {
+            if (!Precision.AlmostEquals(playfield.Clock.CurrentTime, ((ITrack)track).CurrentTime))
+                Logger.Log($@"WARNING: discrepancy in ModTimeRamp; track time was {((ITrack)track).CurrentTime}, playfield time is {playfield.Clock.CurrentTime}");
+
             applyRateAdjustment(playfield.Clock.CurrentTime);
         }
```

Then, crank up user offset to max (+500ms) and start gameplay. You should see the following:

```
[runtime] 2022-05-10 17:27:35 [verbose]: WARNING: discrepancy in ModTimeRamp; track time was 5405.5, playfield time is 5947
```

This proves that the playfield clock applies user offset, which makes it so that you can manipulate the mod's difficulty by messing with offset. I don't think that's good.

Well, that's certainly no longer funny... definitely needs an inline comment mentioning that to avoid potentially changing it in the future, very spooky.

Not entirely sure how to solve this one, but one idea I can think of is providing the gameplay clock as part of `Playfield` and expose the source track clock from there, i.e.:
```csharp
public class Playfield
{
    [Resolved]
    public GameplayClock GameplayClock { get; private set; }
}
...
public class GameplayClock
{
    // source clock of this gameplay clock
    public IClock Source { get; }
}
```
